00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:26.160
Basically, VisualPolitik viewers,&nbsp;
Belarus has one major problem:&nbsp;

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:30.660
From Moscow's perspective, this is not really&nbsp;
a foreign country nor a fully independent one.
 &nbsp;

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:33.960
Instead, they see it as a kind&nbsp;
of autonomous territory that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:37.080
somehow really belongs to Mother Russia itself.
 &nbsp;

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:41.940
And, of course, as you can imagine knowing all&nbsp;
the background, this is extremely disturbing.
 &nbsp;

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:45.540
But the worst thing is that this is not&nbsp;
an exclusively Russian perspective.
 &nbsp;

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:53.460
When Lukashenko came to power in 1994 he&nbsp;
did so by winning an election in which his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:58.980
campaign was based above all on integration with&nbsp;
Russia... Particularly in the economic sphere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:03.180
but not only there. Let's say that, at the&nbsp;
time, Lukashenko received political and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:07.920
economic support from Moscow in exchange&nbsp;
for reinforcing the Russian worldview.
 &nbsp;

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:16.320
In fact, the Belarusian identity, which for years&nbsp;
has been promoted and continues to be promoted by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:22.380
the regime, is based above all on the cult of the&nbsp;
Soviet past, on the "Great Patriotic War" – the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:29.460
Second World War – and brotherhood with Russia.
 
About the rest of Belarus' past... Not a whisper.
 &nbsp;

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:33.420
Lukashenko himself has even said on&nbsp;
state television that Belarusians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:37.860
in essence, are "Russians with a&nbsp;
quality seal". Yes, I kid you not.
 &nbsp;

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:44.340
In fact, it was to a large extent this position&nbsp;
that explains why in 1999 Moscow and Minsk signed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:48.660
a bilateral treaty to progressively&nbsp;
constitute the so-called Union State.
 &nbsp;

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:53.520
In other words, the two countries decided&nbsp;
to merge. Well, not to merge exactly, but to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:58.920
strengthen to the extreme their social, political&nbsp;
and economic integration with joint institutions:
 &nbsp;

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:03.060
We are talking, for example, about a&nbsp;
common currency and a unified tax code.
 &nbsp;

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:10.080
For now, it has not gone that far, but from&nbsp;
the very beginning, freedom of movement between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:14.160
the two countries and the unification of&nbsp;
the two labor markets were established.
 &nbsp;

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:18.660
And so, Russians and Belarusians do not&nbsp;
need permits or any other particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:22.320
documents to work in either country.
 
But... All in all, the question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:26.520
we have to ask is, why did Belarus&nbsp;
decide to take this important step?
 &nbsp;

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:31.440
Let's see, apparently, it had everything to lose,&nbsp;
right? A small country associating with a much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:35.520
larger one... It is clear that Moscow would&nbsp;
end up making all the decisions, isn't it?

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:40.680
Well, actually, no. Or at least that's not&nbsp;
what Lukashenko thought.... Check it out.
 &nbsp;

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:48.960
The 1990s were very hard for Russia, the country&nbsp;
was in a constant crisis and this awakened the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:54.540
ambition of the Belarusian leader. As we have&nbsp;
already told you on several occasions, Lukashenko&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:59.820
came to think that in many joint bodies the weight&nbsp;
of power of Russia and Belarus would be exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:04.920
the same. He even went so far as to think that&nbsp;
he himself could lead this kind of supra-state.
 &nbsp;

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:08.280
Obviously, the passage of time made&nbsp;
things begin to look completely different,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:12.300
to the point that these days we are not&nbsp;
talking about a partnership for Belarus but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:21.300
about the real risk of annexation to Russia.
 
And the question is, was Lukashenko so naïve?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:25.380
How could Europe's last dictator&nbsp;
be a guy who is so easily played?
 &nbsp;

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:30.300
Well, the truth is that this is not quite&nbsp;
the case. The Belarusian dictator always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:36.300
had one eye on Russia and one eye on the&nbsp;
West... Until one day all that changed.
 &nbsp;

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:41.880
So what happened to make Minsk practically&nbsp;
surrender to Moscow? Well, let's take a look.
 &nbsp;

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:53.760
(THE CALCULATED AMBIGUITY)
 
Shortly after the signing of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:58.800
the State of the Union Treaty, the commodity and&nbsp;
oil super-cycle began, which consolidated Putin's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:02.640
first period at the helm of Russia.
 
During those years dollars started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:06.480
flowing into Russia like there was&nbsp;
no tomorrow and, of course, Belarus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:12.060
benefited from that sudden wave of prosperity:
 
Its exports to the Russian giant skyrocketed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:16.740
many young people moved to the neighboring country&nbsp;
to work and, naturally, direct investment by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:22.680
Russian companies in the country multiplied. These&nbsp;
were the golden years of Lukashenko's Belarus.
 &nbsp;

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:28.080
But, as the saying goes, all that glittered was&nbsp;
not gold. There was a problem, a big problem that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:32.400
kept the Belarusian dictator awake at night.&nbsp;
Soon after Putin came to power, Lukashenko&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:37.320
understood perfectly well that the new Russian&nbsp;
leader was not like Yeltsin and that, therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:42.660
his plans to pilot the joint State they had&nbsp;
agreed to create were doomed to absolute failure.

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:51.000
As a result they started dragging their feet on&nbsp;
the project in Minsk. And not only that, when oil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:56.220
prices deflated, the Belarusian government took&nbsp;
the opportunity to stop looking directly to Moscow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.220 --> 00:09:02.280
and to start paying more attention to the West.&nbsp;
The target was the giant European Union market.
 &nbsp;

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:10.260
And that's not even all, it also began to&nbsp;
cultivate a national identity of its own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.260 --> 00:09:15.240
one that went beyond Russia.
 
For example, in 2014 several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:18.540
Russian media outlets began to blame&nbsp;
the Belarusian leader for flirting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.540 --> 00:09:21.840
with nationalism and trying&nbsp;
to supplant Russian culture.
 &nbsp;

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:28.500
("There is an undeclared Russophobic campaign in&nbsp;
Belarus and nationalist forces are being openly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:29.220
pandered to. – Pro-Russian political&nbsp;
commentator Yuriy Baranchik in 2014)
 &nbsp;

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:33.420
The government slyly took all sorts of&nbsp;
measures, some as symbolic as replacing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:37.380
St. George ribbons – which are widely used&nbsp;
at celebrations – with green-red ribbons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:42.540
imitating the colors of the official flag.
 
Also, in 2018, the regime went so far as to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:47.280
allow – and some say even encourage – public&nbsp;
celebrations for the 100th anniversary of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:52.560
the Belarusian republic that was declared in&nbsp;
1918 after temporarily seceding from Russia.

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:57.240
This had been the first attempt at national&nbsp;
independence in its modern history. As you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.240 --> 00:10:02.040
imagine, it’s a symbol for nationalist movements.
 
But these are just two examples of a movement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:06.180
that clearly sought to reinforce a true&nbsp;
national identity for the first time.
 &nbsp;

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:15.180
Not surprisingly, by that time the Belarusian&nbsp;
regime had already become much more cautious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:20.520
about Putin's new Russia. They did not trust&nbsp;
it, they knew that if they did not consolidate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:24.600
a clear national identity and start looking&nbsp;
for commercial and economic alternatives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:29.940
the country would sooner or later be swallowed&nbsp;
up by Russia. They were even willing to give up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:33.960
certain subsidies in exchange for more&nbsp;
economic and political independence.
 &nbsp;

00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:37.800
At that time, beyond appearances, the&nbsp;
truth is that relations between Minsk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:42.900
and Moscow were not exactly the best.
 
In fact, in early 2020 Russia went so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.900 --> 00:10:46.680
far as to cut off oil supplies to&nbsp;
this country and re-established&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:50.760
border controls on the movement of people.
 
To give you an idea of how things stood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:55.260
Lukashenko refused at all times to&nbsp;
recognize the Russian annexation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.260 --> 00:11:02.580
Crimea. For him, Crimea was still Ukraine.
 
And he also made statements like this:
 &nbsp;

00:11:02.580 --> 00:11:07.920
(Belarus would never allow other countries to&nbsp;
use our territory for military intervention in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:09.960
Ukraine – Alexander Lukashenko, 2017)
 
The problem is that looking to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:14.160
West is sometimes easier said than done,&nbsp;
especially if you are a dictatorship with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:18.660
an iron fist and a terribly obsolete economy.
 
In the case of Belarus, all that it could export&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.660 --> 00:11:23.100
to the European Union were basically commodities&nbsp;
and low value-added products: wood, metal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.100 --> 00:11:33.540
potatoes and, above all, petrochemical products.
 
You see, Belarus has a huge installed capacity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.540 --> 00:11:37.140
to produce fuels and oil derivatives. So&nbsp;
what’s the problem? That the whole industry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.140 --> 00:11:42.240
the largest in the country, basically depends&nbsp;
on the supply of cheap Russian oil. And so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:47.460
while it increased its exports to the West,&nbsp;
it increased its dependence on Russia.
 &nbsp;

00:11:47.460 --> 00:11:51.060
It was not an easy situation, but in&nbsp;
any case the truth was that the distance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.060 --> 00:11:56.820
between Moscow and Minsk was increasing.
 
You could say it was the great opportunity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.820 --> 00:12:00.960
to take Belarus out of the Kremlin's sphere&nbsp;
of control once and for all. Nevertheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:06.240
VisualPolitik viewers, in 2020 this&nbsp;
whole process suddenly fell apart.
 &nbsp;

00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:09.840
And no, it was not because of what you&nbsp;
are thinking, the crucial event in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:15.000
whole story was not Covid-19, but the&nbsp;
presidential elections. Check it out.
 &nbsp;

00:12:16.860 --> 00:12:23.640
On 9 August 2020 presidential&nbsp;
elections were held. Lukashenko's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.640 --> 00:12:27.720
candidacy officially won just over 80%&nbsp;
of the vote. Of course, these elections&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:31.920
were almost as rigged as a track and field&nbsp;
race involving King-Jong-un in North Korea.
 &nbsp;

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:37.260
And the result sparked huge peaceful protests&nbsp;
demanding a game change in the country. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.260 --> 00:12:41.160
protests were so large and had so much&nbsp;
international support that for the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:46.320
time the Belarusian regime was on the ropes.&nbsp;
And that's not even all, to make matters worse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:51.480
the Belarusians began to withdraw money en&nbsp;
masse from the banks and tried by all means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.480 --> 00:12:55.680
to convert their local rubles into stronger&nbsp;
currencies, such as the dollar or the euro,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.680 --> 00:13:00.240
in case anything should happen. Within a month&nbsp;
the Belarusians practically exhausted the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:05.220
reserves of the central bank and institutions&nbsp;
such as the IMF refused to come to their aid.
 &nbsp;

00:13:05.220 --> 00:13:09.300
The country was on the verge of collapse&nbsp;
and Lukashenko's dictatorship seemed to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.300 --> 00:13:14.220
hanging by a thread. There was only one way&nbsp;
out: to throw himself into Russia's arms.
 &nbsp;

00:13:16.860 --> 00:13:21.060
And guess what? No sooner said than&nbsp;
done. That's exactly what he did.
 &nbsp;

00:13:24.660 --> 00:13:27.540
The Belarusian regime asked&nbsp;
Vladimir Putin for help,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:32.160
and within days Russia approved&nbsp;
a first bailout of $1.5 billion.
 &nbsp;

00:13:32.700 --> 00:13:39.042
Then, along with that financial bailout,&nbsp;
Russia also promised military assistance....

00:13:39.042 --> 00:13:41.940
(16 August, 2020: Vladimir Putin&nbsp;
has offered to send military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:47.340
assistance to support Belarus President&nbsp;
Alexander Lukashenko as up to 200,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.340 --> 00:13:49.500
protesters gather to demand&nbsp;
his removal – Business Insider)

00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:54.420
On top of that the Kremlin also sent all kinds&nbsp;
of propaganda and repression professionals.
 &nbsp;

00:13:54.420 --> 00:13:57.780
Without going any further, the television&nbsp;
channels were practically taken over by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.780 --> 00:14:03.180
Russian propagandists. Propagandists who were&nbsp;
naturally under orders from Moscow, not Minsk.
 &nbsp;

00:14:03.180 --> 00:14:06.720
Be that as it may, the point is that with&nbsp;
all this help and with the guarantee that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.720 --> 00:14:10.080
the Russian army would prevent any kind of&nbsp;
uprising against the Belarusian government,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:14.700
Lukashenko was able to increase the repression&nbsp;
and persecution of all political opposition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.700 --> 00:14:20.146
without fearing the consequences.
 
In 2021, this even happened, remember?

